--- final message ---
I repaired the two quantitative figures. Two of the nine soft font warnings, on `protocol_overview`, are still open. The paper recompiles to the same 13 pages, so the page budget is unchanged. I didn't rerun the validator or open the recompiled PDF; I only looked at the regenerated PNGs.

**What changed**
- **Fonts:** in `analysis/build_figures.py` I raised titles to 10 pt, axis labels to 9 pt, ticks and legends to 8.5 pt, and bar annotations to 8 pt.
- **Figure height:** I made both figures a little taller to fit the larger text.
- **Tick labels:** in `main_comparison` the x tick labels overlapped at the larger size, so I split them over three lines.
- **PNG size:** the PNGs are now 2675×1306 (`main_comparison`) and 2527×1175 (`error_accounting`), above the 2400 px minimum, and the height warning is gone.
- **DPI exception:** I exported the PNGs at 450 DPI instead of the 300 you specified. At 300 DPI a 5.5-inch figure comes out only 1650 px wide. I didn't enlarge the figure instead, because that would shrink the text when LaTeX scales it to page width. LaTeX still uses the PDFs.
- **Manifest:** I updated the font sizes in `figure_manifest.json` for those two figures, and set their estimated minimum rendered font to 8 pt.

**Still unresolved**
- **`protocol_overview` fonts:** the figure's title and axis fonts are still below minimum (8 pt against 10 and 9). It is a raster concept figure with no plotting source in the workspace, and its width (2560 px) already passes. I left it as is.

I didn't change any data, claims, citations or captions.
